AVM: usb-kbd's usage table follows QEMU's key numbers for F13-F24, the
Korean keys, keypad comma and keypad =

hw/input/hid.c translates the scancodes QEMU hands the USB keyboard
(qemu_input_key_value_to_scancode, that is QEMU's own key numbers, with
e0-prefixed keys in the upper half) to HID usages through a table that
predates those numbers for the rows above: F13 came out as F17, F14 as
F18, F15 as F19, F16-F19 and F21-F24 as nothing, Hangul and Hanja as
nothing, keypad = as F13, and keypad comma (0x7e, a JIS or ABNT2
keyboard's) as the right Windows key, so a tap opened the Start menu and
a held one turned the next keys into Win shortcuts (2026-10-28, fifth
audit). AVM's keyboard for Windows and its own screen both send these
numbers; a screen reader's script on F13 fired the F17 one.

Applied after UTM's own patches/qemu-10.0.12-utm.patch, which doesn't
touch this file.

--- a/hw/input/hid.c
+++ b/hw/input/hid.c
@@ -36,6 +36,11 @@
 
 /* Indices are QEMU keycodes, values are from HID Usage Table.  Indices
  * above 0x80 are for keys that come after 0xe0 or 0xe1+0x1d or 0xe1+0x9d.  */
+/* AVM (2026-10-28): the rows for F13-F24, the Korean keys, keypad comma
+ * and keypad = follow QEMU's key numbers (ui/input-keymap-qnum-to-qcode),
+ * which is what reaches this table: the old rows were for a scancode set
+ * QEMU no longer feeds it, so F13 came out as F17, F16-F19 and F21-F24
+ * as nothing, and keypad comma (0x7e) as the right Windows key (0xe7). */
 static const uint8_t hid_usage_keys[0x100] = {
     0x00, 0x29, 0x1e, 0x1f, 0x20, 0x21, 0x22, 0x23,
     0x24, 0x25, 0x26, 0x27, 0x2d, 0x2e, 0x2a, 0x2b,
